<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Depression</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Depression was the worldwide economic slump that followed the Wall Street Crash of October 1929 and lasted through much of the 1930s. World trade and commodity prices collapsed, causing mass unemployment and bank failures. Export economies such as Chile, dependent on nitrate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, were among the hardest hit, and the crisis destabilised governments across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and Chile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-depression.jpg" alt="Great Depression" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author or not provided · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unemployed_men_queued_outside_a_depression_soup_kitchen_opened_in_Chicago_by_Al_Capone,_02-1931_-_NARA_-_541927.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
